A `func` written inside another function body. `FuncDecl` is one of `Stmt`'s
alternatives, so it parses and checks; the declaration is then hoisted out to
the component that owns the body, because that is what a backend emits from.

The name is the body's, the emitted namespace is flat, and reconciling the two
is what this fixture pins. Before it, the enclosing body was checked twice --
once by preCheckComponentMethods for its signature, once authoritatively by
checkComponentBody -- and the hoist appended a second ir.Func each time. Go got
`method Model.mark already declared`, Kotlin two `fun mark` in one file, and
JavaScript two `function mark` where the second silently won.

`step` and `reset` each write their own `mark`, which is the second half: two
bodies may each declare one name and mean two functions, so the emitted name
carries the body that wrote it. A pure nested func hides both halves --
passInlinePure folds it into its caller before any of this -- which is why
both touch `log`.

Capture is the part that is deliberately absent: the hoist gives the
declaration no closure, so `step`'s `a` is not in scope inside `mark` and
naming it is an error (testdata/error_nested_func_capture.sngl). Both nested
bodies take what they need as a parameter.

deny * `\bmark` -- a nested func is emitted under the body that wrote it (step__mark, reset__mark) and never under the name that body scoped: an identifier *starting* `mark` is one of the two declarations having taken the other's name

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main node {
    var n int = 0
    var log string = ""

    func step(a int) int {
        func mark(k int) int {
            log = log + "!"
            return k * 2
        }
        return mark(a) + 1
    }

    func reset(a int) int {
        func mark(k int) int {
            log = ""
            return k - 1
        }
        return mark(a)
    }

    text(value="{n}{log}")
    button(text="step", @click {
        n = step(n)
    })
    button(text="reset", @click {
        n = reset(n)
    })
}

window {
    main
}
